# The first rows and the last. `head(n)` and `tail(n)` are a row range
# written another way — `head(3)` is the first three rows and `tail(3)` the
# last three — so they go wherever a range goes.
#
# Over a column they give its cells, as a list: `Revenue |> head(3)` is the
# first three numbers. An aggregate over that is one number, so
# `Revenue |> head(3) |> sum()` is the opening quarter without naming the
# months in it, and the same formula holds when a row is added.
#
# Over a sheet they give rows, and a sheet of rows is a sheet: name one
# with `sheet <Name> := <expression>` and it is shown like any other,
# calculated from the sheets it reads and holding no rows of its own. Its
# tab is marked `:=`, as a calculated column's header is. It reaches a
# dataframe-backed table the same way — see `analysis.omx` for one of those.
#
# A cursor is the other way of reaching another row: `Sales[Revenue; *-1]`
# is the row before this one, where a slice is a run of rows taken from one
# end. `shift(n)` says the same over a whole column.

const Opening = Sales.Revenue |> head(3) |> sum()
const Closing = Sales.Revenue |> tail(3) |> sum()

# A slice on its own is the list of what it holds.
const FirstThree = Sales.Revenue |> head(3)
const LastThree  = Sales.Revenue |> tail(3)

# The best of the opening quarter, and how many months there are in all:
# `len` counts rows where `count` counts the values that are not empty.
const BestOpening = Sales.Revenue |> head(3) |> max()
const Months      = Sales.Revenue.len()

sheet Sales

Month | Revenue | Share | Growth
Jan   | 100     | *     | *
Feb   | 120     | *     | *
Mar   | 150     | *     | *
Apr   | 90      | *     | *
May   | 130     | *     | *
Jun   | 170     | *     | *

# Each month against the opening quarter it is measured from.
Share  := (Revenue / Opening).round(3)

# The month before, which a cursor offset gives: `*-1` is one row back,
# empty in the first row where there is no such row, and `//` reads that
# as the month itself so the first growth is zero rather than an error.
Growth := Revenue - (Sales[Revenue; *-1] // Revenue)

# Two sheets calculated from Sales: the first three months, and the months
# that beat a hundred with only their names kept. Neither holds a row of
# its own — the rows are the view the expression gives — so neither takes
# an edit, as a table does not.
sheet Best := Sales |> head(3)

sheet Over := Sales |> filter(Revenue > 100) |> select(Month, Revenue)

# And one calculated from a calculated sheet, which settles after it.
sheet Opened := Best |> head(1)
